    full_text: `In the rainy industrial city of Manchester at the dawn of the nineteenth century, an unassuming Quaker schoolmaster walked every morning to the rooms of the Literary and Philosophical Society on George Street. His name was John Dalton. Dressed in a plain dark coat, knee-breeches, and silver shoe buckles, Dalton was a man of austere simplicity who lived quietly and taught mathematics to schoolchildren for a few pennies an hour.\n\nFor over forty-six years, Dalton kept a daily meteorological diary, recording more than two hundred thousand individual weather observations of wind, rainfall, humidity, and barometric pressure. His obsession with the atmosphere led him to wonder: why do the different gases in the air—oxygen, nitrogen, and water vapor—mix together into one smooth, breathable blend rather than settling into separate layers according to their weight?\n\nTo answer this question, Dalton turned to the ancient concept of the atom. But while ancient philosophers like Democritus and Kanāda had treated atoms as abstract philosophical concepts, Dalton had the practical mind of a chemist and mathematician.\n\nIn his modest workshop, Dalton carved a collection of small solid wooden balls of varying sizes, painted with distinct symbols. He called them his \"billiard ball\" models. In 1803, Dalton announced a revolutionary new doctrine: **The Chemical Atomic Theory**!\n\nDalton’s theory rested on five clear, unshakeable pillars:\n1. All matter is composed of extremely tiny, indivisible, indestructible particles called **atoms**.\n2. All atoms of a given chemical element are completely identical in size, mass, and chemical properties.\n3. The atoms of different elements have different masses and different properties.\n4. Compounds are formed when atoms of different elements combine in simple, fixed, whole-number ratios—such as $1:1$, $1:2$, or $2:3$.\n5. In chemical reactions, atoms are never created or destroyed; they are simply separated, joined, or rearranged into new combinations!\n\nDalton went a step further: he compiled the world's very first table of **Relative Atomic Weights**! Choosing hydrogen—the lightest known substance—as his baseline unit of $1$, Dalton calculated the relative weights of oxygen, nitrogen, and carbon by measuring the proportions in which they combined to form water and gases.\n\nHe published his masterwork, *A New System of Chemical Philosophy*, in 1808. Suddenly, chemistry was no longer a chaotic cookbook of random recipes. It was a precise, mathematical science where every chemical formula could be counted like beads on an abacus!\n\nEven when fame knocked at his door and the greatest academies of Europe showered him with honors, Dalton remained a humble teacher. When King William IV invited him to court, Dalton politely excused himself to finish grading his students’ arithmetic notebooks. John Dalton proved that true genius does not require grand wealth or noisy fanfare—only a steadfast devotion to uncovering the quiet, mathematical building blocks of God’s universe.`,
